# Start Here — Mobile Engineering

Use this documentation to move from **a new developer workstation to a running application, reviewed changes, and verified delivery**. We support React Native, Android, and iOS. **React Native is the preferred starting point for new applications**; follow the assigned stack for an existing application.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep enterprise source, internal links, and operational evidence in approved systems. Never include credentials, signing material, personal data, or sensitive logs in documentation, screenshots, support messages, or AI prompts.

## Choose your starting point

| Your background | Start here | Continue with |
|---|---|---|
| **Web, backend, or another discipline — new to mobile** | [Mobile Foundations](./non-mobile-engineer-guide.mdx): learn how mobile differs from web, including lifecycle, permissions, connectivity, local state, and installed versions. | [Platform Overview](./platform-overview.mdx) → [Machine Setup](./machine-setup-guide/index.mdx) → [First Local Run](./first-local-run.mdx). |
| **Already an Android, iOS, or React Native engineer** | [Platform Overview](./platform-overview.mdx): understand the enterprise tools and ownership boundaries. | [Machine Setup](./machine-setup-guide/index.mdx) → [First Local Run](./first-local-run.mdx). |

**New to mobile? Start with Mobile Foundations.** You do not need to understand Bitrise, signing, or store delivery before learning the basics and running your assigned application locally.

