# Mobile Foundations for Web & Backend Engineers

**New to mobile? Read this page before Platform Overview and Machine Setup.** If you already develop Android, iOS, or React Native applications, continue to [Platform Overview](./platform-overview.mdx).

Your experience with components, APIs, business rules, and tests still applies. Mobile adds a device-controlled lifecycle, native permissions, unreliable connectivity, and installed application versions that do not update together.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic data for examples. Keep enterprise code and internal information in approved systems. Never share credentials, personal data, or sensitive logs in screenshots, support messages, or AI prompts.

## What changes when you move from web to mobile?

| Area | Mobile behavior | What you do differently |
|---|---|---|
| **Application updates** | Users can keep older installed versions. | Maintain API compatibility and handle persisted-data migrations. A repository merge does not update installed apps. |
| **Lifecycle** | The OS can background or terminate the app. Screens can be recreated. | Restore permitted state and reject results belonging to an old screen or session. Never depend on a final cleanup callback. |
| **Connectivity** | A device may switch networks or lose the response to a request. | Show explicit loading, failure, and unknown outcomes. Do not automatically repeat a business write. |
| **Permissions** | Users can deny or later revoke access to notifications and other protected capabilities. | Request permission when the feature needs it and provide a usable denied state. |
| **Local storage** | Data can survive a screen close or app restart. | Separate temporary state from persisted data. Follow account cleanup and approved secure-storage rules. |
| **Credentials** | Application binaries and client configuration can be inspected. | Keep privileged service credentials on protected services. Never embed signing keys or backend secrets. |
| **Navigation** | A notification or external link can open the app from a stopped or active state. | Validate the destination and wait for navigation, authentication, and account readiness. |
| **Delivery** | Builds require platform-specific signing and destination processing. | Distinguish a compiled artifact, a processed upload, and a version actually available to users. |

## Know which platform you are working on

**React Native is preferred for new applications.** Use the assigned stack when joining an existing application; changing it is a team architecture decision.

| Platform | Main application language | Development tools | Important boundary |
|---|---|---|---|
| **React Native** | TypeScript | VS Code, with Android Studio and Xcode for native work | Shared application code still produces separate Android and iOS apps. Native permissions, configuration, signing, and testing remain platform-specific. |
| **Android** | Kotlin | Android Studio | Android lifecycle, resources, manifest configuration, and build variants apply. |
| **iOS** | Swift | Xcode | Apple lifecycle, capabilities, provisioning, schemes, and signing apply. |

Installation instructions for all three platforms live in [Machine Setup — macOS Apple Silicon](./machine-setup-guide/index.mdx).

## Understand a feature before writing it

Use **placing a chicken order in My Chicken App** as a learning example. The names below illustrate responsibilities; they do not define an enterprise API, endpoint, or folder structure.

<MermaidZoom title="A mobile feature — user action, owned state, backend result, and rendering">

