| Responsibility | Chicken-order example |
|---|---|
| **Screen** | Displays quantity, submission progress, and the resulting order state. |
| **State owner** | Coordinates submission and prevents overlapping taps while a request is in progress. React Native uses Redux Toolkit where Redux state is required. |
| **Business rules** | Checks permitted input. The backend independently validates the request and remains authoritative. |
| **Repository** | Uses the established network and persistence abstractions. The screen does not assemble credentials or access storage directly. |
| **Backend** | Authorizes the current user, applies business rules, and records the authoritative order result. |

Do not place every temporary UI value in a global store. An expanded panel can remain screen-local; shared session state needs an application-level owner.

## Handle the mobile interruptions

### The user leaves the screen

Cancel screen-owned work where supported. Before updating UI state, check that the result still belongs to the current owner and account. Cancellation may not stop a request already processed by the backend.

### The order request times out

A timeout does not prove the order failed. The backend may have accepted it before the response was lost. Show an unresolved outcome and use the application's approved reconciliation or idempotency contract before another submission. Do not invent a retry or lookup endpoint.

### The app is terminated

In-memory state can be lost. Restore only data permitted by the application's persistence policy, then reconcile with the backend where required. A single in-memory flag does not provide durable duplicate-order protection.

### The user changes account

Invalidate the previous account's work and clear account-scoped state according to the app's cleanup rules. A late response must not display the previous user's order in the new session.

### Notification permission is denied

The app must remain usable. Notification permission, application preferences, and backend registration are separate concerns. Request permission at an appropriate feature entry point and handle the actual OS result.

## Understand what each verification step proves

| Step | What it proves | What it does not prove |
|---|---|---|
| **Local build and launch** | The selected source and configuration run on the selected target. | Every device works or users can install the release. |
| **Unit and integration tests** | The tested behavior matches the assertions under the tested conditions. | Untested behavior is correct. Even 100% coverage does not establish correctness. |
| **Real-device testing** | Selected journeys work on the tested device and OS combinations. | Every possible device, interruption, or network condition is covered. |
| **Security assessment** | The configured assessment produced findings for the assessed artifact. | All vulnerabilities have been eliminated. |
| **Build success** | The configured build workflow passed. | Signing, distribution, or user visibility succeeded downstream. |
| **Destination verification** | The exact version is available to the intended audience when checked. | Every user has installed or opened that version. |

Detailed implementation and test code belong in Architecture, Platform & SDKs, Push Notifications & Deep Linking, and Testing & Quality. This page introduces the concepts you need to follow those instructions.

**Next:** [Platform Overview](./platform-overview.mdx) — learn which enterprise tools support each part of this journey.






