# Machine Setup — macOS Apple Silicon

Complete the common steps, then your platform tab. **React Native developers complete the Android and/or iOS tab for each target, followed by the React Native tab.** All workstation installation instructions live here.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use approved software sources and access processes. Keep credentials, signing material, private registry configuration, and sensitive diagnostics out of source control and shared messages.

## 1. Get the required versions

Ask the application owner for the assigned repository and its supported tool versions before installing. You can inspect its README and configuration in the repository browser before cloning.

| Tool | Confirm from |
|---|---|
| macOS and Xcode | Project instructions and [Apple's compatibility matrix](https://developer.apple.com/xcode/system-requirements). |
| JDK, Gradle, and Android SDK | Project instructions, Gradle wrapper, Android Gradle Plugin, `compileSdk`, and any daemon JVM configuration. |
| Node and package manager | `.node-version` or `.nvmrc`, `package.json`, package-manager declaration, and committed lockfile. |
| Ruby, Bundler, and CocoaPods, when used | Ruby version file, `Gemfile`, `Gemfile.lock`, and project instructions. |

**If a required version is missing, obtain it from the application owner.** Do not independently select the newest release or change dependency locks to fit your Mac.

Run these commands in Terminal:

```bash
sw_vers
uname -m
sysctl -in sysctl.proc_translated 2>/dev/null || true
df -h .
```

**Expected:** `uname -m` returns `arm64`. A translation value of `1` means the process is running under Rosetta; open a native terminal. Compare macOS and free disk space with the selected tools' requirements.

## 2. Obtain access

| Access | Needed for |
|---|---|
| Enterprise identity, managed-device compliance, and required VPN | Approved internal services. |
| Assigned source repository | Reading code and submitting changes with the appropriate permissions. |
| Private package registries | Projects using private dependencies or SDKs. |
| Build, testing, or monitoring workspaces | Your assigned responsibilities in Vela, Bitrise, Sauce Labs, NowSecure, or Datadog. These are not all prerequisites for a local launch. |
| Apple development access | Physical-device development when required by the project's signing setup. Production signing and store-upload credentials are not general workstation requirements. |

Use the enterprise access process. Have IT supply any required proxy and certificate configuration. A runtime may need its own approved trust configuration even when macOS already trusts the enterprise certificate. Do not disable TLS verification to resolve connection failures.

**Attach screenshot — approved access-request entry point, with sensitive details redacted.**

## 3. Prepare common tools

### Install Apple command-line tools

Check first:

```bash
xcode-select -p
git --version
```

If absent, install through the managed software process or run the following when that installation method is permitted:

```bash
xcode-select --install
```

Complete the installer and repeat the checks. iOS developers also need full Xcode from the iOS tab.

### Configure Homebrew, if used by your team

Use the managed catalog or [official Homebrew installation instructions](https://docs.brew.sh/Installation). Its default Apple Silicon prefix is `/opt/homebrew`.

After installation, check:

```bash
/opt/homebrew/bin/brew --version
/opt/homebrew/bin/brew --prefix
```

For zsh, add this line **once** to `~/.zprofile` using your editor:

```bash
eval "$(/opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv)"
```

Open a new terminal:

```bash
command -v brew
brew --prefix
```

**Expected:** prefix `/opt/homebrew`. Resolve conflicting Intel and ARM installations before continuing.

### Configure Git and verify repository access

Replace both identity values before running:

```bash
git config --global user.name "REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_AUTHOR_NAME"
git config --global user.email "REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_AUTHOR_EMAIL"
git config --get user.name
git config --get user.email
```

Use repository-local identity instead if your work requires multiple identities. Authenticate through the approved HTTPS credential helper or SSH process. Do not place tokens in clone URLs.

Copy the assigned repository's approved clone URL from its browser page:

```bash
MOBILE_REPO_URL='REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_CLONE_URL'
git ls-remote "$MOBILE_REPO_URL" HEAD
```

**Expected:** authentication succeeds and an initialized repository returns a commit and `HEAD` reference. A new empty repository may return no reference. This read-only check does not prove push permissions.

## 4. Install your platform tools

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

### A. Install Android Studio and SDK components

1. Install the project-supported **Mac Apple Silicon** build through the managed catalog or [official Android Studio instructions](https://developer.android.com/studio/install).
2. Complete the Setup Wizard and open **SDK Manager**.
3. Record the SDK location. Install the SDK Platform matching the project's `compileSdk`, Platform-Tools, and Android Emulator.
4. Install Command-line Tools if using `sdkmanager`. Add specific Build Tools, NDK, or CMake versions only when the project requires them.
5. Install a supported **ARM64** emulator image. Choose Google Play support when the test requires it.

**Attach screenshot — SDK Manager showing the required components and SDK location.**

### B. Select the compatible JDK

Check the project's supported combination using [Android's JDK guidance](https://developer.android.com/build/jdks). Select the supported Gradle JDK in Android Studio, accounting for any project-managed daemon JVM criteria.

For terminal builds, add the confirmed JDK configuration once to `~/.zshrc`. This bundled-runtime example applies **only if its version matches the project** and Android Studio is installed at this location:

```bash
export JAVA_HOME="/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home"
export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH"
```

Otherwise use the actual home of your approved JDK installation. Do not point `JAVA_HOME` at its `bin` directory. IDE and terminal builds can select Java differently; align both with the project configuration.

### C. Configure and verify SDK tools

Add this block once to `~/.zshrc`, replacing the SDK location if SDK Manager shows a different path:

```bash
export ANDROID_HOME="$HOME/Library/Android/sdk"
export PATH="$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools:$ANDROID_HOME/emulator:$ANDROID_HOME/cmdline-tools/latest/bin:$PATH"
```

If Command-line Tools uses a versioned directory rather than `latest`, use the installed path. Open a new terminal:

```bash
java -version
printf '%s\n' "$JAVA_HOME" "$ANDROID_HOME"
adb version
emulator -version
```

