# First Local Run & Contribution

Follow this page after [Machine Setup](./machine-setup-guide/index.mdx). First run the assigned application unchanged. Then make a small, agreed change, verify it, and submit a pull request.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use approved test environments and accounts. Keep secrets, production data, private artifact URLs, and sensitive screenshots out of commits, PRs, support messages, and AI prompts.

## 1. Obtain the assigned source

Get the repository URL, starting branch, development environment, and expected launch screen from the application owner. Replace every `REPLACE_WITH_...` value before using a command.

Run from your approved local development directory. The `mobile-app` destination must not already exist:

```bash
MOBILE_REPO_URL='REPLACE_WITH_APPROVED_CLONE_URL'
MOBILE_SOURCE_BRANCH='REPLACE_WITH_ASSIGNED_SOURCE_BRANCH'
git clone --branch "$MOBILE_SOURCE_BRANCH" "$MOBILE_REPO_URL" mobile-app
cd mobile-app
git status --short --branch
git rev-parse HEAD
```

**Expected:** the assigned branch is checked out with no local changes. Record the commit for any setup investigation. If you already have a checkout, inspect it first; do not reset or overwrite existing work.

**Starting a new application instead?** Use the approved template's documented generator and version, then enter the generated application directory. The template repository root is not necessarily the application. Confirm the generated files and lockfiles before continuing. Do not substitute an unpinned public generator. Source creation and publication are separate from Bitrise enrollment.

## 2. Configure the development environment

Read the repository's setup instructions before creating configuration files.

| Input | Action |
|---|---|
| **Tool versions** | Activate the project's Node, Java, Ruby, and Xcode selections as applicable. |
| **Private dependencies** | Use the approved registry authentication mechanism. Do not place tokens in manifests or clone URLs. |
| **Environment** | Select the documented development configuration and endpoint. Do not use production as a setup shortcut. |
| **Local files** | Use only filenames and keys the project actually reads. An arbitrary `.env` file does not automatically configure an app. |
| **Test identity** | Obtain an approved test account through the team's credential process. |
| **Target** | Confirm the Android module/variant or iOS workspace/scheme and supported simulator or emulator. |

For an actual local configuration file, check whether it is ignored and whether it is already tracked:

```bash
MOBILE_LOCAL_CONFIG='REPLACE_WITH_LOCAL_CONFIG_PATH'
git check-ignore -v -- "$MOBILE_LOCAL_CONFIG"
git ls-files -- "$MOBILE_LOCAL_CONFIG"
git status --short
```

No output from `check-ignore` means no matching ignore rule was found. Output from `ls-files` means the file is tracked; adding an ignore rule will not remove it from Git. Follow the repository's configuration policy before placing any sensitive value in a file.

## 3. Build and open the application

Choose your platform. The task names, paths, and script names below are conditional examples; use the ones documented by your application.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

### A. Inspect the project tasks

Open the Gradle root in Android Studio: the directory containing `gradlew` and the settings file. Let synchronization complete. In Terminal at that root:

```bash
./gradlew --version
./gradlew projects
./gradlew tasks --all
```

**Expected:** Java and Gradle match the project requirements, and the intended application module and development tasks exist.

### B. Build and run the development variant

For a project with module `app` and variant `debug`:

```bash
./gradlew :app:assembleDebug
adb devices -l
```

For another module or flavor, use the actual task from the project. See [Android's command-line build reference](https://developer.android.com/build/building-cmdline).

In Android Studio, select the correct development variant, application run configuration, and running emulator, then choose **Run**.

**Expected:** the intended app installs and opens. An assemble task alone only produces an artifact; it does not launch the app. Review Logcat if launch fails.

**Attach screenshot — application running on the selected Android emulator, with sensitive details redacted.**

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

### A. Resolve the declared dependencies

For Swift Package Manager, open the documented project/workspace and follow its committed package-resolution policy.

For a CocoaPods project with a root `Gemfile` and `ios/Podfile`, run from the application root:

```bash
bundle check || bundle install
(
  cd ios
  bundle exec pod install
)
```

Adjust the directory to the actual layout. Use the selected Ruby and locked Bundler/CocoaPods versions. Use `pod install`, not a blanket `pod update`, for onboarding; [CocoaPods explains the difference](https://guides.cocoapods.org/using/pod-install-vs-update.html).

### B. Build and run in Xcode

1. Open the documented `.xcworkspace` for a CocoaPods integration, or the project's designated workspace/project for another setup.
2. Select the development scheme and configuration.
3. Select a supported iOS Simulator.
4. Choose **Product → Run**.

**Expected:** Xcode builds, installs, and opens the app. If a scheme or runtime is missing, check the selected workspace and Xcode rather than changing signing settings. See [Apple's run instructions](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/running-your-app-on-simulated-or-physical-devices).

To inspect a known workspace's schemes from Terminal:

```bash
MOBILE_IOS_WORKSPACE='REPLACE_WITH_WORKSPACE_PATH'
xcodebuild -list -workspace "$MOBILE_IOS_WORKSPACE"
```

For an `.xcodeproj` without a workspace, use the actual project path with `-project` instead.

**Attach screenshot — application running on the selected iOS Simulator, with sensitive details redacted.**

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

### A. Install locked dependencies

At the application root, confirm `package.json`, the committed lockfile, and the required Node version:

```bash
node --version
node -p 'process.arch'
```

Use **one** installation command matching the repository's declared package manager:

| Project uses | Locked installation command |
|---|---|
| npm with `package-lock.json` | `npm ci` |
| Modern Yarn | `yarn install --immutable` |
| Yarn Classic | `yarn install --frozen-lockfile` |
| pnpm | `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` |

Follow any workspace-specific instructions. A manifest/lock mismatch needs investigation, not lockfile deletion. [npm documents `npm ci` requirements and behavior](https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v11/commands/npm-ci/).

For an iOS target using CocoaPods, also perform the Pod installation from the iOS tab. JavaScript dependency installation does not complete native setup.

### B. Start the application using its scripts

Inspect available scripts:

```bash
node -e 'console.log(JSON.stringify(require("./package.json").scripts, null, 2))'
```

If the project defines `start`, `android`, and `ios`, and npm is its package manager, use these examples. Otherwise use its actual scripts and documented target arguments.

Terminal 1, at the application root:

```bash
npm run start
```

Start the required emulator or simulator. In Terminal 2, at the same root, run the selected target:

```bash
npm run android
```

Or, for iOS:

```bash
npm run ios
```

Some scripts start Metro automatically; follow the project's workflow to avoid duplicate servers. Select the documented variant, scheme, and target. Keep development-server access within the approved network.

**Expected:** the native app opens, loads its JavaScript bundle, and displays the intended development screen without an unhandled exception. For dual-platform work, verify both Android and iOS.

Keep the established Redux Toolkit state setup. Do not introduce another state library as a launch fix.

**Attach screenshot — React Native app running on each target in scope, with sensitive details redacted.**

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 4. Verify the first run

| Action | Expected result |
|---|---|
| Open the app | The intended app and environment appear without a fatal error. |
| Use the approved test sign-in, if required | The documented signed-in state appears. |
| Open the agreed feature | Expected test data or the documented empty state is shown. |
| Close and reopen the app | Session and restoration behavior match the application's design. |
| Inspect the working tree | Unexpected configuration, dependency, and generated-file changes are understood before committing. |

```bash
git status --short
git diff --stat
```

For a My Chicken App learning exercise, use a sandbox order journey only when the owner provides that environment and its expected behavior. Do not place a real order to verify onboarding.

| Failure | Check first |
|---|---|
| Dependency authentication or certificate error | Registry entitlement, credential binding, required VPN, and runtime trust configuration. |
| Gradle task missing | Correct root, module, variant, and available tasks. |
| iOS dependency or scheme missing | Correct workspace, completed dependency installation, shared scheme, and selected Xcode. |
| React Native bundle unavailable | Actual scripts, Metro process, development build, and target connectivity. |
| API unreachable or wrong initial screen | Device routing, endpoint, account, environment, and expected test data. |

If blocked, share the exact failing step, source commit, relevant versions, and sanitized error through [Teams Support](../support/support.mdx). Preserve the original error; do not start by deleting locks, upgrading dependencies, or disabling security controls.

## 5. Make your first change

Agree on one small task and its expected behavior with your team. Examples include a localized label correction or a missing validation rule. Follow [Branching & PR Conventions](../architecture/branching-prs.mdx).

Application feature/fix PRs target **`main`**, use **Conventional Commits**, and require **two peer approvals, a passing Danger check, and a passing Bitrise build**, together with applicable required checks. Configuration-repository enrollment promotion is a different process.

With a clean working tree and an approved remote named `origin`, fetch and create a new task branch from current remote `main`. Replace the example task identifier and description according to the documented naming convention:

```bash
git status --short --branch
git fetch origin
MOBILE_WORK_BRANCH='feature/REPLACE_WITH_TASK_ID-description'
git switch --no-track -c "$MOBILE_WORK_BRANCH" origin/main
```

Use `fix/REPLACE_WITH_TASK_ID-description` for a fix. If the working tree is not clean or the branch already exists, inspect the situation before continuing; do not reset or overwrite it. Run the affected baseline checks if the branch's source differs from the version you first launched.

Implement within the existing architecture. Add meaningful regression tests for the changed behavior and relevant failure cases. Review any AI-generated code and tests before committing them.

## 6. Run checks for your change

Use the repository's actual commands and inspect the reports. These are examples, not guaranteed project tasks:

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

For a standard `app` module and `debug` variant, from the Gradle root:

```bash
./gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest :app:lintDebug :app:assembleDebug
```

Run the repository's separate coverage evaluation and required instrumentation tests. These three tasks alone do not prove coverage or real-device behavior.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

For a test-enabled shared workspace scheme and available Simulator:

```bash
MOBILE_IOS_WORKSPACE='REPLACE_WITH_WORKSPACE_PATH'
MOBILE_IOS_SCHEME='REPLACE_WITH_TEST_ENABLED_SHARED_SCHEME'
MOBILE_SIMULATOR_UDID='REPLACE_WITH_AVAILABLE_SIMULATOR_UDID'
xcodebuild \
  -workspace "$MOBILE_IOS_WORKSPACE" \
  -scheme "$MOBILE_IOS_SCHEME" \
  -destination "platform=iOS Simulator,id=$MOBILE_SIMULATOR_UDID" \
  -enableCodeCoverage YES \
  test
```

Adapt to the actual workspace/project and test plan. Run required lint and coverage evaluation too. Coverage collection does not enforce the required threshold.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

If npm is the assigned package manager and these scripts exist:

```bash
npm run typecheck
npm run lint
npm run test:coverage
```

Use the actual scripts otherwise. Inspect coverage scope and reports, then build and run affected native targets. JavaScript tests do not establish native integration correctness. React Native Android native-device testing follows the approved JUnit-based setup; use Testing & Quality for execution details.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

**Required coverage: 100% for production code under the supported metrics defined in Testing & Quality.** Account for every production file, use the documented treatment of unsupported metrics, and follow the approved basis for any exclusions. Do not weaken thresholds or replace meaningful assertions with coverage-only tests. A required check that did not run must be reported as blocked, not passed.

## 7. Commit and submit the PR

Review before staging:

```bash
git status --short
git diff --check
git diff
```

Replace the sample paths with the intended source and test files, then review exactly what will be committed:

```bash
git add -- path/to/changed-source-file path/to/changed-test-file
git diff --cached --check
git diff --cached
```

Commit using the documented convention. This message is a synthetic example; use a message describing your actual change:

```bash
git commit -m "fix(order): reject an invalid chicken quantity"
git push --set-upstream origin HEAD
```

Open the PR against `main`, use the repository's template, and add the required assignee and reviewers. Explain the problem, resulting behavior, exact checks and outcomes, measured coverage, and anything blocked. Add redacted screenshots only when useful.

**Attach screenshot — first PR showing its description, requested reviewers, and current status checks, with sensitive details redacted.**

Respond to review, rerun affected checks after updates, and obtain the required approvals and current-revision results. Merge through the approved process or hand off to an authorized maintainer. A pending check is not passing; a merged PR is not a verified release.

**Continue:** [Architecture](../architecture/index.mdx) for feature design, or the relevant platform standards for implementation. Use CI/CD when you need enrollment or delivery instructions. [Teams Support](../support/support.mdx) · [FAQ](../support/index.mdx).







